  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_n84StRzstN92gw7y",
   "name": "Calculating Electrical Power",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Calculating electrical power"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Power is defined as the **rate of doing work**\n- Potential difference is the **work done per unit charge**\n- Current is the **rate of flow of charge**\n- Therefore, the power dissipated (delivered) by an electrical device is defined as:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$P=IV$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $P$ = power, measured in W\n  - $I$* *= current, measured in A\n  - $V$ = potential difference, measured in V"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Resistance is given by the equation:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$R=\\frac{V}{I}\\RightarrowV=IR\\RightarrowI=\\frac{V}{R}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $R$ = resistance, measured in Ω\n  - $V$ = potential difference, measured in V\n  - * *$I$* *= current, measured in A\n- The expression for potential difference can be substituted into the power equation to give:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`P space equals space I V space equals space I open parentheses I R close parentheses space`"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$P=I^{2}R$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Or the expression for current can be substituted into the power equation to give:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`P space equals space I V space equals space open parentheses V over R close parentheses V`"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$P=\\frac{V^{2}}{R}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This shows that for a given resistance, if the current or potential difference were doubled, the power would be four times greater"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Two lamps are connected in series to a 150 V power supply.\n\n![WE - power question image, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes](assets/796e9f7cf777-we-power-question-image.png)\n\nWhich statement most accurately describes what happens?\n\n**A.** Both lamps light normally\n\n**B.** The 15 V lamp blows\n\n**C. **Only the 41 W lamp lights\n\n**D.** Both lamps light at less than their normal brightness\n\n**Answer:** **A**\n\n**Step 1: State the power equation**\n\n*P = IV*\n\n**Step 2: Rearrange for *****I***\n\n$I=\\frac{P}{V}$\n\n**Step 3: Substitute in values to calculate**\n\n- For the 41W lamp:\n\n** **$I=\\frac{41W}{135V}=0.3A$\n\n- For the 4.5W lamp:\n\n** **$I=\\frac{4.5W}{15V}=0.3A$\n\n- For both to operate at their normal brightness, a current of 0.3 A is required\n- Since the lamps are connected in series, the same current would flow through both\n- Therefore, the lamps will light at their normal brightness\n- This is option **A**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "You can use the mnemonic “Twinkle Twinkle Little Star, Power equals *I* squared *R*” to remember whether to multiply or divide by resistance in the power equations. Which equation to use will depend on whether the value of current or voltage has been given in the question."
  }
 ]
}